Approach 2: Better (Explicit Left & Right Search)
Intuition

For every index:

Find first smaller element on left.
Find first smaller element on right.

Still uses nested loops.

Java
class Solution {

    public int largestRectangleArea(int[] heights) {

        int n = heights.length;
        int maxArea = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {

            int left = i - 1;

            while (left >= 0 && heights[left] >= heights[i])
                left--;

            int right = i + 1;

            while (right < n && heights[right] >= heights[i])
                right++;

            int width = right - left - 1;

            maxArea = Math.max(maxArea, width * heights[i]);
        }

        return maxArea;
    }
}

Complexity
Time : O(n²)
Space : O(1)
Approach 3: Monotonic Stack (LSR + RSR) ⭐ (Your Code)
Intuition

For every bar,

find

Nearest Smaller to Left
Nearest Smaller to Right

using two monotonic stacks.

Then

Width = Right Smaller − Left Smaller − 1

Area = Width × Height
Step 1 : Find Left Smaller

For every bar,

find the first smaller element on the left.

Java
public static int[] heightsLSR(int heights[]) {

    int n = heights.length;

    int ans[] = new int[n];

    Stack<Integer> st = new Stack<>();

    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {

        while (!st.isEmpty() &&
               heights[i] <= heights[st.peek()]) {

            st.pop();
        }

        ans[i] = st.isEmpty() ? -1 : st.peek();

        st.push(i);
    }

    return ans;
}

Step 2 : Find Right Smaller

Java
public static int[] heightsRSR(int heights[]) {

    int n = heights.length;

    int ans[] = new int[n];

    Stack<Integer> st = new Stack<>();

    for (int i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {

        while (!st.isEmpty() &&
               heights[i] <= heights[st.peek()]) {

            st.pop();
        }

        ans[i] = st.isEmpty() ? n : st.peek();

        st.push(i);
    }

    return ans;
}


